add(
'The support theorem is valid for the stated model: if F=M_t B_t^-1 ... B_1^-1 M_0 and each B_i has at most s nonzeros per row while M_i are monomial, then F^-1 has row support at most s^t. A dense inverse Fourier matrix requires s^t>=N. The integer loop computes ceil(log_s N) without floating logarithms. The random routine tracks Boolean possible support, so numerical cancellations cannot invalidate its upper bound.',
'support_lower_bound asserts s>=2,N>=2, but not integrality. random_row_sparsity_audit has no explicit domain checks for N,s,t. For positive N and s>N, its while len(choices)<s can never finish: choices are contained in range(N). N=0 reaches max(empty), negative t silently performs no stages, noninteger s has unclear rounded behavior.',
'Lower-bound loop uses O(log_s N) multiplications. Random support storage can grow to N² elements; naive set unions per factor can take O(N·s·min(N,s^level)) work. Sampling s distinct values becomes expensive as s approaches N. It is a dense structural audit, not an O(N) Green application.',
'Three support-bound values and four finite random cases appear in phase15 final test class. None covers s>N, N=0, or negative t, and that entire class is omitted in direct script invocation. No tests run here.',
'Standard-library random; math imported unused. No unreviewed local dependency.',
[{'severity':'high','lines':'21–35','finding':'Deterministic nontermination for s>N: at most N distinct indices can ever enter choices, so the while loop cannot terminate. This is a source proof, not an executed failure.'},{'severity':'info','lines':'1–8','finding':'The source correctly excludes sums of Green solves and stages with nonsparse inverses. Applying this support veto to those models would exceed its mathematical scope.'}],
'Accept the sparse-inverse product support argument and small valid-domain Boolean controls. Repair input guards before treating the random helper as a general callable audit.')
